/**
 * @mainpage Ankad Load Balancer
 *
 * @section intro Overview
 * A C++ discrete-time simulation of a company load balancer. Random web
 * requests (source IP, destination IP, processing time, job type) arrive in
 * a FIFO queue, and a LoadBalancer hands them to a pool of WebServer objects.
 * Time is measured in clock cycles.
 *
 * @section classes Main classes
 * - Request: one web request (struct).
 * - RequestQueue: FIFO queue of requests.
 * - WebServer: processes one request at a time.
 * - LoadBalancer: owns the queue and servers, keeps the clock, dispatches
 *   work, and scales the number of servers.
 * - IPBlocker: firewall that rejects blocked IP ranges.
 * - Config: settings read from config.txt.
 * - Logger and Stats: colored console output, log file, and summary counters.
 *
 * @section scaling Dynamic scaling
 * The load balancer keeps the queue between 50 and 80 requests per server.
 * Above 80 x servers it adds a server; below 50 x servers it removes an idle
 * one. After each change it waits scale_cooldown cycles before checking again.
 *
 * @section usage Build and run
 * @code
 * make
 * ./loadbalancer
 * @endcode
 * Edit config.txt to change settings without recompiling.
 */
